I reviewed `jadx-core`'s string utilities and made one clean, behavior-preserving structural refactoring.

## What I changed (`jadx-core/.../jadx/core/utils/StringUtils.java`)

`getSpecialStringForCodePoint(int)` and `escapeWhiteSpaceChar(char)` each hand-wrote the **same five whitespace escape mappings** (`\n`→`\\n`, `\r`, `\t`, `\b`, `\f`). That's duplicated knowledge in two methods feeding two different escape paths (Java string-literal escaping vs. XML/resource escaping via `commonEscapeAndAppend`) — if someone adjusts one, the other silently drifts.

I made `escapeWhiteSpaceChar` the single source of truth. `getSpecialStringForCodePoint` now keeps only its unique cases and delegates the rest:

```java
private String getSpecialStringForCodePoint(int c) {
    switch (c) {
        case '\'':  return "'";
        case '"':   return "\\\"";
        case '\\':  return "\\\\";
        default:
            // whitespace escapes shared with escapeWhiteSpaceChar;
            // guard the cast so a supplementary code point can't be truncated into one
            return c <= Character.MAX_VALUE ? escapeWhiteSpaceChar((char) c) : null;
    }
}
```

## Correctness notes
- **The guard matters.** The source method takes an `int` code point, the target takes a `char`. Without `c <= Character.MAX_VALUE`, a supplementary code point like `0x1000A` would cast down to `0x000A` (`\n`) and get wrongly escaped. For in-range non-whitespace chars, `escapeWhiteSpaceChar` returns `null` — exactly the old `default`. So behavior is identical across all inputs.
- I deliberately **did not** "modernize" `notBlank`'s `trim().isEmpty()` to `isBlank()` — they aren't equivalent (`\b` is trimmable by `trim()` but is not `Character.isWhitespace`), so that would be a silent behavior change.
- Braces balanced; net −8 lines.

As before, the module isn't buildable in this environment (no network/cached Gradle deps), so I verified by inspection. Combined with last turn, the working tree now has uncommitted changes in `Utils.java` and `StringUtils.java`. Not committed — say the word if you'd like me to.

